# Line endings.
#
# Windows and Linux end a line of text differently. The build scripts read a
# file and write it back, so running them on a non-Windows machine rewrites
# every line ending in the file. The visible text is unchanged, but git
# compares line by line and reports the whole file as rewritten. One new
# article then looks like thirteen articles rewritten, and the history stops
# being useful.
#
# This file stops that happening. Git stores every text file with one
# convention internally and hands the working copy the right one for whatever
# machine it is checked out on, so the scripts can run anywhere.
#
# ONE-OFF STEP after adding this file. Check what git is doing today:
#
#     git config --get core.autocrlf
#
# If that prints "true", nothing further is needed: the repository is already
# stored the way this file expects and you will see no change.
#
# If it prints "false" or nothing at all, run these two commands on their own,
# with no other edits pending, and commit them as a change of their own:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git commit -m "Normalise line endings"
#
# That produces one large commit touching every text file. It is a one-off and
# it is the whole point: after it, the problem cannot come back. Keeping it in
# its own commit is what stops it burying a real change.

* text=auto

# Binary formats. Never convert these; doing so corrupts them.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.docx  binary
*.pptx  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
